  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Supporting Humanitarian Responders with Access to Hard to Reach Locations to Operationalize Life-Saving Activities</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">After more than 13 years of conflict, violence and insecurity, north-east Nigeria continues to remain a large-scale humanitarian and protection crisis, affecting the lives, livelihoods and wellbeing of millions of people in the region. Year 2021 saw a rising upsurge of attacks against civilians by non-state armed groups (NSAG) creating new waves of displacement. In Borno State, several attacks, ambushes and abductions by NSAG continued along the main roads. Humanitarian access continues to be a challenge to all humanitarian actors in trying to reach vulnerable populations in the north-east, due to the ongoing security situation. Despite efforts by the Nigerian military, the insurgent attacks continue and in 2022 humanitarian workers have often been targeted. Since 2016, IOM has been managing humanitarian hubs in eight locations across Borno State. These hubs play a critical enabling role in the provision of life-saving humanitarian assistance by improving access to the more than 7 million people in need of assistance in north-east Nigeria. Through this project, IOM continues to support humanitarian responders with access to accommodation incl. services such as meals, laundry, conference/training room, social and recreational areas, offices and internet connectivity through the Emergency Telecommunications Sector (ETS).</narrative>
  </description>
